    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-37953",
      "knowledge_point_id": "16601513-9b74-5cec-9af6-2d99b98e1ab3",
      "knowledge_point": "Evaluating a Repeated Integral",
      "problem": "${\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({2}^{y+1}{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}({2}^{y+1}{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{2}^{y+1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{2}^{y+1}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{2}^{y+1}(\\frac{{1}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{0}^{3}}{3})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{{2}^{y+1}}{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{{2}^{y+1}}{3}\\,\\text{d}y & ={[\\frac{{2}^{y+1}}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{2+1}}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-\\frac{{2}^{1+1}}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-4}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "\\frac{4}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}"
