      "knowledge_point_id": "d8f012d0-444a-5035-8a72-a053de312d63",
      "knowledge_point": "Changing the Order of Integration",
      "problem": "${\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}(x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,{[xy+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[(2x+\\frac{8}{3})-(x+\\frac{1}{3})]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\frac{7}{3})\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\frac{7}{3})\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{7x}{3}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{7}{3})-(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{7}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
